# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Confidential. Must never enter the repository.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Somewhere to put anything that cannot be shared. Drop it here and it is safe.
private/
secret/

# Internal working notes, kept on this machine only: the conversation log with
# the advisor, how to present the work, and lessons carried from earlier work.
docs-md/

# Handover documents and contracts from earlier work. They contain screenshots
# of a running system, hostnames and client document titles.
#
# These patterns have to spell out the names they exclude, which is the one place
# in this repository where those names appear. Anything under private/ is already
# covered above; these are a second line for files that land elsewhere by
# accident. Remove them only after confirming nothing outside private/ needs them.
#
# The Thai patterns below stay Thai: they match Thai filenames, so translating
# them would stop them matching anything. They are the only Thai left in this
# repository, and deliberately so.
*เอกสารส่งมอบ*
*Neo?Chat*
*NeoChat*
*สัญญา*
*แบบตรวจสอบ*
NEOCHAT-*

# Plant documents under NDA.
storage/
manuals/
*.pdf
!docs-md/**/*.pdf

# The one exception, deliberately narrow (ADR-033). The synthetic corpus has to
# be committed because ADR-019 makes a document's sha256 its id, so generating
# the files at test time would tie every id to a PDF library version. It is not
# in a directory called manuals/ so that the rule above keeps applying to
# anything that is.
#
# The two files are named rather than globbed. A glob re-includes anything with
# a .pdf extension dropped into that directory, and the only thing that would
# notice is a test in CI - by which point a real manual has already been pushed
# to GitHub. Named, an unexpected PDF stays ignored and cannot be committed at
# all, and adding one to the corpus is a deliberate edit here.
!fixtures/corpus/r-2000ic-maintenance.pdf
!fixtures/corpus/m-710ic-maintenance.pdf

